# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOTES (not part of the diff -- read before applying)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# WHAT THE BUG IS
# ----------------
# `main()` sets `backend_mock = None` and never changes it, so
# `worker_qiskit`'s call was effectively `transpile(circuit, backend=None,
# optimization_level=3)`. With no backend AND no basis_gates, transpile()
# has no target basis to translate to -- confirmed directly (see below):
# every `UnitaryGate` in the circuit passes straight through completely
# untouched, at every qubit count tested. This is the same class of "looks
# fast because it does no work" issue this project has already caught and
# retracted once before (the 1000-qubit "615x-867x" figures, per the
# README's "What we haven't verified yet" section) -- except this time it
# affects the QISKIT side of a comparison instead of PSF-Zero's.
#
# CONFIRMED DIRECTLY (this project's sandbox, same circuit construction as
# phase2.py's own build_dense_pair_blocks_circuit(), 20 gates/pair):
#
#     qubits | input ops         | output ops after transpile(backend=None, optimization_level=3)
#     156    | {'unitary': 1560} | {'unitary': 1560}   <- byte-identical, nothing happened
#     300    | {'unitary': 3000} | {'unitary': 3000}   <- byte-identical
#     1000   | {'unitary': 10000}| {'unitary': 10000}  <- byte-identical
#
# WHY THE ORIGINAL RUN STILL SHOWED A FLAT ~1.5s "QISKIT" TIME
# --------------------------------------------------------------
# transpile() pays a real, one-time warmup cost the first time it's called
# in a fresh Python process (building its internal preset PassManager,
# loading stage plugins, etc.) -- confirmed directly: a trivial 4-qubit
# circuit took 0.475s on the first call in a fresh process and 0.005s on
# every call after that, in the same process. Because phase2.py's
# `run_with_monitor()` spawns a brand-new `multiprocessing.Process` for
# EVERY worker invocation (and Windows always uses the 'spawn' start
# method, so every one of those processes re-imports everything from
# scratch), every single "Qiskit" measurement in the original run pays this
# warmup tax freshly -- while the actual transpile work being timed does
# nothing at all, per the table above. The reported ~1.5s "Qiskit" number
# is measuring interpreter/library cold-start cost, not compilation work,
# and does not scale with qubit count because neither the warmup cost nor
# the (nonexistent) transpile work does.
#
# WHAT THIS PATCH DOES
# ----------------------
# Gives Qiskit an actual target basis (`rz`, `sx`, `x`, `cx` -- an ordinary
# universal gate set, matching what `compile_for_hardware()`'s own
# `basis_gates` fix targets elsewhere in this project) so `transpile()` is
# forced to actually decompose every `UnitaryGate` block, making it do
# equivalent real work to what PSF-Zero's `compile()` is doing. Confirmed
# in this project's sandbox that this produces qubit-count-dependent
# output and qubit-count-dependent time (0.140s at 156 qubits, 0.511s at
# 1000 qubits -- unlike the flat, workless original).
#
# This patch does NOT fix the warmup-cost-per-subprocess issue -- for a
# clean timing comparison, consider calling `transpile()` (and
# `psf_compile.compile()`) once on a small throwaway circuit inside each
# worker, before starting the timer, to pay any one-time cost outside the
# measured interval; or restructure to reuse one long-lived worker process
# per compiler across all seeds/sizes instead of spawning fresh each time.
